--- A top-level variable read before its first assignment, and assigned later,
--- passes the lowering: the interpreter stops when it runs, and the compiled
--- program does not build.
---
--- crow run -i:  runtime error: line 19: undefined variable 'n'   (exit 1)
--- crow run:     error[E0425]: cannot find value `n` in this scope
---                 --> src/main.rs:36:36
---               error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler
---               refused what Crow accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program
--- crow build --ir accepts it (status 0) and prints the IR.
--- Expected: refused before the program runs, in both modes, as the same read
--- is when the name is never assigned (`log[n]` alone: "1:5: undefined variable
--- 'n'") and as a function's local read before its assignment is ("undefined
--- variable 'm'", both modes, before running). The same with a bare `n` line in
--- place of `log[n]`. Found writing rosetta/idiomatically_determine_all_the_
--- characters_that_can_be_used_for_symbols.co, whose probe program
--- `zq` / `zq = 7` (a name split by a line break) was accepted by build --ir.

log[n]
n = 7
log[n + 1]
